<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aspathines</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Aspacanā, Aspathínēs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aspathines (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> Aspacanā) was a senior official of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> who served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>; he was born and died sometime between 550 and 450 BCE. He is depicted on the tomb of Darius I at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a>, where an inscription names him as the bowbearer who holds King Darius' battle-axe. The only other courtier named there with a dedication was Gobryas. His son Prexaspes became an admiral in the navy of Xerxes during the second Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aspathines.jpg" alt="Aspathines" width="330" height="746" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Darius_I_Aspathines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Six of them descended from the nobles who joined Darius in 522 BCE to kill the man he called the usurper Gaumata: Otanes, Intaphernes, Gobryas, Hydarnes, Megabyzus and <b>Aspathines</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
